Date: Mon, 19 Jul 93 08:41:42 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: What are big upcoming problems?

In regard to projections on manufacturing:

If you keep up with the manufacturing world, like I do, you will
find steady but not radical progress in productivity going on, which
explains the decrease in manufacturing employment.  I read 'Modern
Machine Shop', which is one of those free-to-engineers publications
that is 90% advertisements.  The kinds of improvements I see come
from, for example, milling machines with automated drill bit changeout.

This reduces the amount of human labor to make a part, since the
manual changing of bits is eliminated.  Another big improvement is
computer drive of the machine tools coupled with CAD software that
generates the tool paths automatically.  In the past couple of
years the pieces of software and hardware have been integrated to
the point that you can design a part, bolt a block of metal onto
the carrier plate, click on 'make part' on the screen, and an
hour later come back and find a finished part.  The role of the
traditional blue-collar worker is nearly gone, replaced by a
CAD guy.

Since these automated machines run in the $100,000 range per unit,
you don't see machine shops changing over instantly.  What you have
is small shops installing one automated machine to handle a major
product line (like turning out gears of various sizes), and keeping
their other machines around for the rest of their work.  A big
manufacturer may install a number of machines when they build a
new plant (like Boeing did for it's new parts fabrication plant in
Auburn WA).  But the time scale is probably 20 years for the automated
milling machines to fill the niche they have an advantage in.  During
this time (which we are already in) I expect something like a 
halving of manufacturing jobs relative to other jobs, as productivity
goes up.

The problem, if there is one, is what do all those unemployed
blue collar workers do?  One answer is nothing - if the jobs
reduction is slow enough then it can be accommodated by retirement.
What, after all, did we do with all those farmers who used to make
up most of the working population?  The answer, again, is nothing.
Most farmers stayed on their farms and retired/died.  Most of their
children went to the city to work.  The absolute number of farmers
has not declined that drastically, since the population as a whole
has grown.  And the productivity of farmers is enormously higher
than it used to be.

Dani Eder

