Date: Mon, 19 Jul 93 10:18:07 -0400
From: hhuang@athena.mit.edu
Subject: DIET: tryptophan, bananas, chromium, phenylalanine, etc.

Given the fascist ban on L-tryptophan by the FDA, I've been wondering
what the best ways may be to experiment with getting some serotonin
into my brain.

If anybody knows of any sources which still sell tryptophan, please
email me.  CERI's _Smart Drugs News_ noted that tryptophan can now be
ordered from England and gave an address as well.  Does that mean I
need a prescription to get it?  Or can I just order it?

By the way, _Smart Drugs II_ by Dean and Morgenthaler is out and can
be ordered from CERI.

I've read that a heavily carbohydrate meal will raise insulin levels
which will decrease amino acid levels in the blood, and will help
tryptophan get into the brain where it is made into serotonin.  Hence
why carbohydrate meals may cause one to be more relaxed or sleepier
afterwards.

I've also read and heard from various sources that bananas have a lot
of tryptophan.  Yet, a post to usenet that listed many sources of
tryptophan listed bananas as containing a minimal amount of
tryptophan.  All listed sources of meat and other animal products
contained more tryptophan than bananas did.  (I read it in usenet, so
it *must* be true. :) I would appreciate any pointers for information,
or else it's a search in the library for me.

But eating those animal products is not the best way to get
tryptophan, because almost all good sources of tryptophan also contain
high levels of other amino acids, which will crowd out the tryptophan
in the brain's uptake of amino acids.  This is why a high protein meal
is considered mildly excitatory, because the tyrosine (and presumably
phenylalanine as well) can make its way up into the brain and be
converted into dopamine and norepinephrine.

However, this is not to downplay one's intake of tryptophan from those
sources.  If one doesn't have enough tryptophan in the first place,
then one won't be able to shunt enough into the brain no matter what
one does.

Now a few questions here.  First of all, proposition: even a good diet
cannot deliver the 200 mcg that one small capsule of chromium
picolinate can deliver.  Furthermore, a diet high in refined grains
and sugars will leech chromium from the body.  Hence, if you can
believe that most people are chromium deficient, then you can believe
that most people have a low glucose tolerance factor, and hence their
insulin response to carbohydrate intake is probably skewed as well.
So if one is adequatedly supplemented with chromium, will this
carbohydrate->insulin->lower blood amino acid levels->tryptophan
->serotonin pathway work as well?  And perhaps bananas are considered
a good source of tryptophan because bananas are high in carbohydrates,
and hence a good way to get tryptophan into the brain?

I will vouch that 200 mcg of chromium from the picolinate form has
help stabilize my blood glucose levels.  My energy level shifts used
to be more severe along with my blood glucose.  For example, I used to
eat a big meal only to feel very hungry only three hours later, so
hungry that I couldn't, say, concentrate in lectures during the
semester.  I had noticed these effects before I began making
significant changes to my diet, and also before I experimented with
DL-phenylalanine in the mornings, since L-phenylalanine is supposed to
be an appetite suppressant.

Finally, despite chromium and whatnot, I've noted that high
carbohydrate meals at night do have a mildly calming effect.
Sometimes, the effect is obvious.  On Saturday evening while talking
with Jay O'Connell and Alex Chislenko at a party at Ron's (can't
recall last name) house in Somerville, Massachusetts, I grazed on high
carbohydrate vegan and felt sufficiently relaxed afterwards that I
talked little.  Yet, sometimes, I don't feel anything at all compared
to the usual evening winding-down effects.

Of course, nothing can make me as sluggish as overeating can.

Note: psychological willpower will generally beat out all these
non-hard-drug mood-altering effects.  For example, TwinLab's
GABA-inositol-niacinamide capsules do indeed have an inhibitory
sleep-inducing effect for me.  But I've been able to barrel on past
them and stay up as long for as I wanted to.  And, of course,
stimulants can't stimulate you unless you are willing to play along
psychologically.  If I'm too self-pitying to get out of bed and face
the day, all the L-phenylalanine won't be able to do it for me either.

I still must always decide *myself* how to go about my life.

One last example.  I've found that as long as I've had enough sleep,
and it's a waking hour, and I'm not injured, and my stomach isn't too
full -- that no matter how depressed or fatigued or otherwise
resistant I am -- that I've *always* had more than enough energy to go
get up and exercise.

As Michael Colgan wrote in _Your Personal Vitamin Bible_ regarding
exercise (paraphrased),

``The body is always willing, it is the spirit that is weak.''

-Han Y. Huang                                    hhuang@athena.mit.edu

