to exi-bulk).

> I hypothesize that our brains are not genetically built such that
> it requires irrational religion to utilitize them fully. This is the 
> hypothesis I wish to see tested.

May I alter that?  How about
"Suspension of rationality can[not] serve the goals of rationality."
This looks like what we're ultimately interested in.

>   I do not believe we are testing hypothesis A). We want to test
> whether channeling gods are any better than role-playing Michael Jordan. I
> am not disputing the usefulness of rituals, I am disputing the
> usefulness of religious symbols over others.

Oops, I'm confused.  In the first quote, you seemed to be talking
about whether irrationality was useful at all.  Or maybe we have a
difference of opinion as to the rationality of channeling Michael
Jordan?  I'd call this arational at best, myself.

As to the choice of symbols, what exactly is a "religious symbol"?
If it is simply a symbol used in religious practice, can it then be
taken and used in a non-"religious" way, or is there some
ineradicable religiosity?

Dave Krieger said (thanks for the delurk, btw):
> >Group 1 performs established, traditional rituals in accordance with
> >accepted practice.  These rituals would be prescribed and supervised by an
> >experienced practitioner.
> >
> >Group 2 would practice pop-culture rituals such as the ones Mark describes,
> >made up out of whole cloth especially for the occasion.  As Mark made clear
> >in his post, they should be as slipshod and corny as possible, because we
> >want to test the assertion that _any_ ritual will do, right?
> >
> >Group 3 would practice no ritual, but would approach the efficacy test with
> >no special preparation.

I think we have a series of questions:
1) Does ritual work?
2) For a given group, does one ritual work better than another?
3) Are there cross-practitioner differences between ritual systems?
(for example, one might conceivably find that everybody tested gets
more priapism out of Pan than out of Nyarlathotep, or that only Xists
can get the water-walking bit down pat.)

The first could be tested by splitting a homogenous population and
applying Group 1 and Group 3 procedures.  The second would involve a
comparison of Group 1 and Group 2, with a control group thrown in
for good measure.  Both hypotheses could thus be tested by running
all three groups, though this would sacrifice a bit of statistical
power on the first question.  The third is not a hypothesis, but
more of an invitation to look for or invent patterns in the results.

