ritual invocation leaps to mind.)  My brain's working slowly, I've jsut
noticed the similarity to the old witch-doctor's trick: if you dress up in
the correct clothing, and produce an obscure scroll, which another
curiously-clothed person will convert into some salve, unguent or tablet,
the patient will quite probably get better. 

The problem is not just to test whether ritual works, which gets into
nasty objective/subjective debates (Well, _I_ saw the Great God Pan, even
if you didn't) which we can, however, work around, but to eliminate the
added factor of `is it working or not working because of the
preconceptions of the participants'.

I would suspect that the approach to take would involve checking 
beforehand what the expectations of the participants were, if they were
told what they were getting into. Then, afterwards, check if the
participants believed it was going to work. Only problem is ensuring
they're telling the truth. (Discreet questioning separately from someone
who obviously believes in it, an arch-skeptic, and someone who's
impartial?) 

Of course, continuing this classification through into the later
experiments would get even more interesting; if belief in a ritual were
shown to enhance its effects, which is plausible since it is a known
technique in certain of the trades (medicine!), then it might be possible
to find even more interesting classifications of rituals, which would
offer more information for deducing how they work, which in turn helps
tell is more about how the brain works.

Damn, I'm interested. Who's going to sort out the experiments?

