    they are well-matched to each other (The Spider-Man Theme Song Ritual
    will work, but it's got to be the right ritual for the Spider-Man Theme
    Song...)
 
My own prejudice (based on my own experimentation in this area) is that E
is actually more often correct than the other two.

Does anybody besides me remember the magic word SHAZAM used in the Captain
Marvel comics?  It stood for the various "entities" from whom Big Red
supposedly derived his powers.  However, back in 1988 I noted that (with a
little re-arrangement) they were a good match for the first six "circuits"
(agents or subminds) of Leary's eight-circuit model for the mind, as
presented in Robert Anton Wilson's "Prometheus Rising" (which I know, as
good Extropians, you've all read, right? :-) [Meta-note:  Yes, I knew what
various people meant by "EC" in posts on another thread, but I object to
the term "Extropically correct" as silly.]:
                                Wilsonian circuit:
S = Solomon     Wisdom          3rd -- symbol-manipulating intelligence
H = Hercules    Strength        2nd -- mammalian pack dominance
A = Atlas       Something       1st -- biosurvival, "the weight of the world"
Z = Zeus        Something else  6th -- the metaprogrammer, "King of the Gods"
A = Achilles    Invulnerability 5th -- neurosomatic; mind-body integration
M = Mercury     Speed           4th -- Hermes Trismegistus, socio-sexual

(If anyone is interested, I can elaborate on why these seem, to me, to be
good correspondences.  Note:  In _PR_, Wilson reverses the order of the
sixth and seventh circuits from every other published reference he or Leary
makes to this model.  I am using the non-PR order for these circuits.)

For quite a few months I got a lot of mileage out of using these
characters, in self-hypnosis and other kinds of meditation, as metaphors
for those aspects of the operation of the brain.  How does this reconcile
with the superficially- obvious observation that taking the objects of
one's concentration from a COMIC BOOK, f'cryinoutloud, is certifiably
loopy?

This comes back to why I think the design of an effective ritual depends on
congruence between the symbol being contemplated and the ritual in which
that contemplation is embedded.  *YOU HAVE TO BE ABLE TO TAKE IT
SERIOUSLY*.  I was able to take this comic-book imagery seriously because,
even though that's where it came from, by the time I was finished loading
it with my own meanings and my own interpretation, it was something quite
different.  (NOTE:  This SHAZAM mythos is *NOT* the Minsky-Skinner
technique that I'm working on now... SHAZAM was one of my first crude
efforts back in the late 80's.)

Let me re-iterate for the record:  if you think I ascribe any physical
existence to Zeus, Achilles, or Mercury anywhere outside of my own head,
you're nutty.  These characters are _memes_, nothing more than figments of
the human imagination.  However, Freedom, Science, and the Future are just
memes, too, and yet lots of people (perhaps including some on this list)
pretend that these concepts exist apart from human thought and language,
and have fought and died for those "gods".

(If you take exception to that statement, is it because you believe
Freedom, Science, and the Future are "real", and that I have said they are
not?  Memes _are_ real, in the same sense that Microsoft Windows is real,
or your memories of your childhood are real... but they only exist as
patterns of information, variously represented, and not as physical things
themselves.)

The useful role of personifications is as handles for manipulating subsets
of your mind's agents.

> Later Dave points out that we need to have some result to measure. That's 
> where I got hung up. Outside of brick-breaking and fire-walking I can't
> think of any objective behavior we can measure. Which is not to say that
> no one else can. 

I think the measurement of objective behavior is (partially) a wrong turn. 
Read the rest of this before you start composing your flames, folks; this
requires some elaboration.

Ask anyone experienced in this area and they will tell you that X (where X
stands for whatever brand of self-metaprogramming they practice) is most
effective at changing the _internal_ landscape -- emotions, beliefs,
attitudes, motivations, and desires.  [There are more appropriate
technologies for almost any objective task anyway.  If you want to walk
across fire and break bricks, why not use asbestos boots and a jack-hammer?
 But we don't have any jack-hammers that can break through depression or
asbestos boots that can protect us from self-doubt (hold on a sec while I
turn down the purple-prose knob... ah, that's better).]  We have no
objective "handle" on these memetic phenomena yet (no "happy-meter", no
"optimism-builder-upper"), and, until we have a ***much*** better
understanding of the brain than we do, the only tools we have to deal with
them are more memes.  Today there is no way, except your own reporting, for
us to measure or observe changes in your mind.

Does this make such changes meaningless -- that is, not subject to
scientific discourse?  A's internal states may not be scientifically
testable by B, since B has no way to refute any statement A may make about
them, but they aren't meaningless to A.  Nor are they insignificant to B,
if A is someone B cares about.  Remember, 100% of the data we have
available is filtered through our perceptions, which in turn are controlled
by memetic factors -- we see what we expect to see, and we delete out
(pre-consciously) sense-data that don't fit our internal model.  Remember
the experiment with the banana that was seen as a knife by all of the
experimental subjects because the person holding it was using it to "stab"
somebody else.

Where the emotions are involved, *a placebo effect is a real effect*.  If I
perform the Holey Bendover Ritual on you before you go to a job interview,
and it makes you feel more confident, does it make any sense to say that
you only "thought" you felt more confident, or that you were "deluded" into
thinking you were more confident, when "really" you weren't?  What if
someone prays to Jesus in a stressful time, and they feel a great weight
lifted from their heart and they feel happier?  Is there any meaning in the
statement that their relief is illusory and they're "really" still
worrying?  Your emotions "really are" as you perceive them to be --
restated as a tautology: you have no emotions apart from the way you feel
:-)

So, if the benefit someone expects to derive from some system of ritual
practice is primarily emotional or perceptual, you're going to have a tough
time measuring it aside from their own reports.  Does this mean that
science has to retire from the game?  Yes, except for self-experimentation
(Your reports of your internal states may not be objective, but at least
they're first-hand :-), _unless_ you're willing to assume, for the purposes
of the dialogue, that your correspondents are in fact representing their
internal states as they experience them; otherwise, you have no data to
use.

This happens in physical science all the time.  If a physicist or chemist
doubts the numbers that a colleague has gotten out of an experiment, he
doesn't swoop down on the other guy's lab with a SWAT team and ferret out
his notebooks; he repeats the experiment himself, and sees if he gets
results that are similar within the bounds of statistical significance. 
It's more valid (and more persuasive) to say "I did this experiment and got
different results" than "I accuse Dr. Jacobo of cooking his books".

This brings up another problem with scientific study of internal phenomena
-- replicability of results.  *The desire to get results* is the single
greatest determinant of success with techniques of self-metaprogramming. 
Honest practitioners admit this.  In physical science, this is
unforgiveable; we (rightfully) laugh PK and ESP researchers off the stage
when they claim "These mechanisms won't work in the presence of skeptics." 
However, in the realm of belief, it is very well established that memetic
phenomena respond to memetic stimuli and the pre-existing memetic
environment.

If you doubt my results in measuring, say, the critical temperature of my
new ceramic superconductor, you can go through the same procedures I
describe and, even if you are skeptical, if you follow the same procedures,
you will get the same results.  However, in this type of experiment, your
skepticism is external to the mechanism under study.

If, on the other hand, you doubt that I am more motivated and more
optimistic as a result of doing the Hokey-Pokey for five minutes every
morning before breakfast, it is more difficult for you to duplicate the
experiment, in a skeptical frame of mind, because the mechanism under study
-- your mind -- is itself the organ of skepticism.  It's as if the
superconductor you're studying is itself a component in the thermometer
you're using to study it.

The ability of the mind to accept new memes depends on the underlying
foundation of pre-existing memes, and _memes affect the behavior of the
mind_.  If I am attempting to install a new belief in the minds of A and B,
it makes a difference if A's mind includes the meme "It's easy to change my
beliefs," and B's includes the meme "It's impossible to change my beliefs."

This is the reason why I think this topic is so vital to Extropianism: 
because Dynamic Optimism is fundamentally about the _self-regulation of
one's beliefs_.  We have taken an important step toward achieving Goal X
when we have come to believe that Goal X is achievable (we will certainly
make no progress toward goals we believe impossible, because, rationally,
we see that our efforts are more leveraged elsewhere).  Fortunately, in my
experience, it is possible to instill the meta-meme "Beliefs are easy to
change" by engaging in a well-designed course of mind games.

> I am not disputing the usefulness of rituals, I am disputing the
>usefulness of religious symbols over others. (e.g. I don't believe
>they are more special than any others. Whether or not they are
>powerful for you depends on your value system and probably your
