Right, but how do you test this without raising all of your experimental
subjects from infancy?  Most people in this society were raised in an
environment in which their parents and elders provided an example of
reverence for (some kind of) religious symbols.  Few of us, I'm sure, had
parents who prayed to Wonder Woman or grandparents who were buried in the
Church of Rambo.  The pre-existing _internal_ memetic environment
prejudices the receptivity of the mind to particular sets of symbols.

What about people who adopt a religion (or change religions) in adulthood? 
(A clue can be found in Antero Alli's book "A Modern Shaman's Guide to
Reality Selection," which makes at least one valid point:  the ritual you
design for yourself has the potential to be much more effective _for you_
than just about any ritual somebody else can design, because it _means_
something to you.)  I'm sure a large part of the motivation for those folks
who stagger from one cult to another is the search for _meaning_.  That set
of "receptor sites" (the one that says, "Okay, this is the _truth_, you can
stop thinking and worrying now") is either vacant or not sufficiently
bonded to whatever meme scheme is presently sitting there.

I guess this gets back to different implicit assumptions that were made
before I got mixed into this.  Are we talking about what symbols are
more/less useful for _humans in theory_, or symbols and their effectiveness
for _humans as they exist today_?  I had assumed we were discussing
real-world people, not people raised in a symbol-neutral environment.  My
contention is that, for most people, religious memes are pre-loaded with
significance and contain the most bang for the buck for the purpose of
initiating brain change.

